417] [17592186560991 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/0a/0a438012465859bc6406bb52a6bebeb13803760b429996fc7e4963b415c8f23d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb374c99d9da28e40ad87dda6bf86dc6ca273a491e4faccb7dee043a104d0eba.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/a5/a58fac3f854cd89429090b7fd10140d26d2097372586cd5f130353bff338b97c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/74bbb3351cf6720121c6ce0fbe8f99fa1c65560edbe5e547ecfd007835a50b89.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b9e706a-7ed7-5d91-bf85-9f21697ce716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560996 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/17/173a69de844fac6141fe2fb3c695d070750b9df29b91364f66a0c1da28998428.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d60800fb2b986aac396a278599651b9ee116339d4b5bbab9a7a6ae5e16f6a3b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,0)\\cup \\{1\\}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary  point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b96b52d-bf1b-5917-8deb-641e04d4e427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561001 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/b5/b57ab5bfd2f0d685e2918c2f62601b9439475e7f29e2fbdabb912c43411a829f.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a2dc9c52c0c24b2f611cfd3b5aa23fb068249780fd66d80039b7d548c6151a.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b47e92adb22c8d68cf94f947870d185ef907f9ca17c7427843e03fbcdab21cc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2-x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,1)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d9ea65f-7a2f-51be-a1e0-0db423dddcbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561031 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2-x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ab/ab319d348d61ba7d247b21553de0625f8c4f9906800102a6f8e11264472cab13.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(2,1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebf116e1093aa4861de907ed9256ec52e4a5b3e0765a6ab66cc8ee0b40e1769.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb3ee0d5-b81d-5d00-99b5-5160f6c70200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561043 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the left of the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8f/8f182873a8384097b56152284e67189e36f0a79a0b2bbda8926a2f8279db7ddd.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,1)$ and $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66c8cb59cceadd43e3c4316925f1dd624c2e77a6ec547c9f9a1a02846bb537fb.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality $y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
